0222: EFB copy readbacks: check the memory as the game left it when it issued the copy

Bug (user crash, 2026-10-14, LM at 120 fps interpolation with a busy CPU): a readback of an EFB copy
was written into memory whenever that memory still matched what Aurora saw when it executed the copy.
With frame interpolation Aurora executes a frame's copies at the frame's first replay, on the render
thread, while the game already runs ahead; on a starved CPU that replay can come well after the game
issued the copy. At a map change the game frees the old map's buffers (the 640x480 Z16 shadow map
copy, 600 KB, read back every frame) and loads the new map into the same heap: the late copy then
hashed the new contents as "before the copy", the readback landed, and the old depth map was written
over the new map's data (logged 14 times in 40 warps in a starved test run).

aurora_efb_ram_guest_copy(dest, w, h, fmt), called by the game thread at GXCopyTex, records the hash
of the range at that moment; the readback's pre-copy hash is that one (the host hash stays the
fallback for copies the game did not announce). A writeback lands only if the game has not written
the range since issuing the copy (or the memory is still our previous writeback), which is the state
the console would be in. Stats: staleSkipped (readbacks the old check would have written over new
contents), lateChanged; both also logged (first 20).

